1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:08,722
We ran the three most popular five-minute scalping rules on 117,000 crypto futures trades, and a coin flip did just as well.

2
00:00:09,243 --> 00:00:14,537
After fees, out of 93 coins, the best-known rule made money on exactly one.

3
00:00:15,078 --> 00:00:16,582
This is Lumin Futures.

4
00:00:17,043 --> 00:00:21,033
We test trading ideas on real futures data, with the fees left in.

5
00:00:21,554 --> 00:00:23,539
This is not an opinion about scalping.

6
00:00:23,920 --> 00:00:30,757
It is six months of Binance futures candles, the same exits for every rule, and the fee charged on every single trade.

7
00:00:31,138 --> 00:00:33,825
Here is what the data showed, and why it happens.

8
00:00:34,347 --> 00:00:42,447
We took every five-minute candle from the first of May to the end of October, 2026, for 93 perpetual futures pairs on Binance.

9
00:00:42,888 --> 00:00:48,723
Bitcoin, Ethereum, Solana, and 90 other coins, straight from Binance's own public archive.

10
00:00:49,084 --> 00:00:53,254
Then we coded the three rules you will find in almost every scalping tutorial.

11
00:00:53,775 --> 00:00:58,307
One, the moving average cross, the 9 EMA crossing the 21 EMA.

12
00:00:58,888 --> 00:01:05,906
Two, the RSI reversal, buying when RSI climbs back above 30 and selling when it drops back below 70.

13
00:01:06,447 --> 00:01:10,858
Three, the MACD cross, when the MACD line crosses its signal line.

14
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:13,946
All three get the filter every tutorial adds.

15
00:01:14,387 --> 00:01:18,157
Only buy above the 200 EMA, only sell below it.

16
00:01:18,598 --> 00:01:21,545
And then a fourth strategy that should not work at all.

17
00:01:21,966 --> 00:01:22,688
A coin flip.

18
00:01:23,149 --> 00:01:28,042
Random candles, a random direction, traded just as often as the moving average rule.

19
00:01:28,523 --> 00:01:31,531
Every trade, from every rule, is managed the same way.

20
00:01:31,972 --> 00:01:34,338
The signal has to appear on a closed candle.

21
00:01:34,719 --> 00:01:38,628
We enter at the open of the next candle, so nothing peeks into the future.

22
00:01:38,969 --> 00:01:43,721
The stop goes one average true range away, the target one and a half ranges away.

23
00:01:44,202 --> 00:01:47,571
If price touches both inside one candle, we count the loss.

24
00:01:48,012 --> 00:01:50,980
We charge 0.07% round-trip.

25
00:01:51,340 --> 00:01:56,273
That is Binance's standard rate when one side gets a maker fill and the other is a market order.

26
00:01:56,714 --> 00:02:03,952
Real scalpers using market entries and stop-losses often pay 0.10%, so this test is actually generous.

27
00:02:04,393 --> 00:02:07,922
So the only thing that differs between the four strategies is the entry.

28
00:02:08,423 --> 00:02:09,787
That is the whole question.

29
00:02:10,228 --> 00:02:12,253
Does the entry signal know anything?

30
00:02:12,654 --> 00:02:15,622
Before fees, here is the average result per trade.

31
00:02:15,983 --> 00:02:19,892
The moving average cross, −0.004%.

32
00:02:20,374 --> 00:02:21,637
MACD, zero.

33
00:02:22,339 --> 00:02:25,627
RSI, −0.006%.

34
00:02:26,088 --> 00:02:29,777
And the coin flip, −0.005%.

35
00:02:30,198 --> 00:02:31,722
Those differences are noise.

36
00:02:32,063 --> 00:02:37,677
Draw the statistical range around each one and they all overlap, and all of them sit on zero.

37
00:02:38,158 --> 00:02:46,259
Over 117,000 trades, the most popular entry signal on a five-minute chart did exactly what a random entry did.

38
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:47,702
Look at the win rates.

39
00:02:48,103 --> 00:02:50,670
All four win about 40% of the time.

40
00:02:51,091 --> 00:02:52,755
That is not a coincidence.

41
00:02:53,196 --> 00:03:00,856
With a target one and a half times farther than the stop, price that moves at random reaches the stop first about 60% of the time.

42
00:03:01,377 --> 00:03:05,768
Forty percent wins is exactly what no edge looks like with this setup.

43
00:03:06,369 --> 00:03:08,956
Here is what that looks like on one real trade.

44
00:03:09,357 --> 00:03:13,387
Bitcoin, the first of July, 2026, on the five-minute chart.

45
00:03:13,888 --> 00:03:19,242
At 1:40 a.m., UTC, the 9 EMA crosses above the 21.

46
00:03:19,723 --> 00:03:20,746
A textbook long.

47
00:03:21,227 --> 00:03:22,771
But look at where it fires.

48
00:03:23,232 --> 00:03:28,545
Bitcoin has already climbed about 870 dollars off its low in the last half hour.

49
00:03:29,006 --> 00:03:32,315
The signal only arrives after the move it is reacting to.

50
00:03:32,956 --> 00:03:37,488
The trade enters at the next open, 58,872.

51
00:03:38,009 --> 00:03:42,621
The stop sits one average range below, the target one and a half above.

52
00:03:43,142 --> 00:03:48,135
Twenty minutes later, a single wick dips through the stop by less than a third of a range.

53
00:03:48,616 --> 00:03:50,080
Ordinary noise for that hour.

54
00:03:50,521 --> 00:03:53,568
The trade is out, down 0.32%.

55
00:03:54,050 --> 00:03:54,611
Then the fee.

56
00:03:55,092 --> 00:03:57,177
Minus 0.39%.

57
00:03:57,679 --> 00:04:01,408
And by 3:50, Bitcoin trades straight through the old target.

58
00:04:01,929 --> 00:04:03,313
The idea was not wrong.

59
00:04:03,694 --> 00:04:08,807
The entry was late, the stop sat inside the noise, and the fee was charged either way.

60
00:04:09,529 --> 00:04:11,574
Now charge the fee on every trade.

61
00:04:12,055 --> 00:04:19,333
Every strategy loses, about 0.07% per trade, because that is the fee and there was no edge to pay it.

62
00:04:19,935 --> 00:04:30,080
Stack every trade the moving average rule took across all 93 pairs together, without compounding, and the fees alone come to more than 82 full position sizes.

63
00:04:30,501 --> 00:04:32,847
The total loss is more than 87.

64
00:04:33,369 --> 00:04:35,073
It was not one bad month either.

65
00:04:35,554 --> 00:04:40,286
Every month from May to October was negative for the moving average rule after fees.

66
00:04:40,787 --> 00:04:47,023
Coin by coin, after fees, the moving average rule was profitable on one pair out of 93.

67
00:04:47,564 --> 00:04:48,747
MACD on two.

68
00:04:49,389 --> 00:04:52,417
RSI on 13, from far fewer trades.

69
00:04:52,878 --> 00:04:54,442
The coin flip, on none.

70
00:04:55,023 --> 00:04:57,409
Here is the part most scalping videos skip.

71
00:04:57,930 --> 00:05:02,141
A fee is a fixed cost, but a five-minute trade is a small move.

72
00:05:02,602 --> 00:05:07,635
On these coins, the average five-minute trade moved about half a percent from entry to exit.

73
00:05:08,036 --> 00:05:11,304
So the fee ate about 13% of the average move.

74
00:05:11,805 --> 00:05:16,477
Run the same rules on 15-minute candles and the fee is about 8% of the move.

75
00:05:16,878 --> 00:05:19,184
On one-hour candles, about 4%.

76
00:05:19,505 --> 00:05:21,309
But be careful with what that means.

77
00:05:21,690 --> 00:05:24,197
The slower charts did not rescue these rules.

78
00:05:24,537 --> 00:05:28,708
On 15 minutes and one hour, the same entries still lost after fees.

79
00:05:29,109 --> 00:05:31,234
A slower chart makes the fee smaller.

80
00:05:31,595 --> 00:05:33,801
It does not create an edge that was never there.

81
00:05:34,362 --> 00:05:35,786
One rule did clear the fee.

82
00:05:36,367 --> 00:05:43,245
RSI on the one-hour chart, by about 0.01% per trade, over 841 trades.

83
00:05:43,585 --> 00:05:47,355
The statistical range on that runs from a clear loss to a clear gain.

84
00:05:47,696 --> 00:05:48,718
That is not an edge.

85
00:05:48,979 --> 00:05:50,623
That is not enough trades to know.

86
00:05:51,185 --> 00:05:52,548
Three honest limits.

87
00:05:53,029 --> 00:05:55,235
First, these results are optimistic.

88
00:05:55,576 --> 00:06:01,310
We gave every trade a perfect entry at the next open and no slippage, and real fills are worse.

89
00:06:01,791 --> 00:06:07,887
Second, we tested today's list of coins across six months, and coins that died along the way are not in it.

90
00:06:08,368 --> 00:06:16,809
Third, these are the textbook rules, not any one trader's personal system, and this says nothing about a skilled trader reading the chart by hand.

91
00:06:17,230 --> 00:06:20,338
What it does show is narrower and harder to argue with.

92
00:06:20,719 --> 00:06:28,479
The entry signals that most tutorials teach, on the timeframe most tutorials use, carried no measurable information about the next move.

93
00:06:28,799 --> 00:06:32,709
And on that timeframe, the fee is a large slice of every trade.

94
00:06:33,090 --> 00:06:36,699
If there is no edge in the entry, only two things change the result.

95
00:06:37,040 --> 00:06:40,288
The cost of each trade, and the size of each trade's move.

96
00:06:40,729 --> 00:06:43,516
A limit order that rests on the book pays the lower fee.

97
00:06:43,958 --> 00:06:52,018
A trade planned around a level before price gets there, instead of a reaction after a candle closes, can aim for a larger move with the same risk.

98
00:06:52,479 --> 00:06:53,702
Neither is a promise.

99
00:06:54,123 --> 00:06:56,609
They are the two things this test says matter.

100
00:06:57,131 --> 00:07:01,582
Next, we test pre-planned limit levels on the exact same six months of data.

101
00:07:02,023 --> 00:07:04,529
Subscribe to see if the maths finally flips.

102
00:07:04,991 --> 00:07:06,534
This has been Lumin Futures.
